                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg2.l12.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span><span id="alg2.l12.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">end</span><span id="alg2.l12.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="alg2.l12.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg2.l13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">end</span><span id="alg2.l13.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="alg2.l13.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">while</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Algorithm and Implementation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the results above, below comes the training and sampling algorithms. The model design is illustrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.1 Forward ‣ 4 Continuously Augmented Discrete Diffusion (CADD) ‣ Continuously Augmented Discrete Diffusion model for Categorical Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> regarding how the model is trained and how it handles one sampling step.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training Loss</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">According to equation <a href="#S4.E14" title="Equation 4.14 ‣ Lemma 1. ‣ 4.2 Reverse ‣ 4 Continuously Augmented Discrete Diffusion (CADD) ‣ Continuously Augmented Discrete Diffusion model for Categorical Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.14</span></a>, the model aims to learn to maximize the likelihood of discrete path, and also minimize the reweighted MSE in equation <a href="#S4.E16" title="Equation 4.16 ‣ Lemma 1. ‣ 4.2 Reverse ‣ 4 Continuously Augmented Discrete Diffusion (CADD) ‣ Continuously Augmented Discrete Diffusion model for Categorical Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.16</span></a>. Inspired by continuous diffusion models that used for categorical modeling, e.g., CDCD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dieleman et al., 2022</a>)</cite> and Plaid <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gulrajani &amp; Hashimoto, 2023</a>)</cite>, we may estimate <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\bm{z}}}_{0,\theta}:=\sum_{v}p_{\theta}(\hat{\bm{x}}_{0}=v\mid{\bm{x}}_{t},{\bm{z}}_{t})\,{\bm{w}}_{\theta,v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\bm{z}}}_{0,\theta}:=\sum_{v}p_{\theta}(\hat{\bm{x}}_{0}=v\mid{\bm{x}}_{t},{\bm{z}}_{t})\,{\bm{w}}_{\theta,v}</annotation></semantics></math> and just train the model to predict correct categorical output to minimize the KL divergence. Thus, we choose to train CADD by minimizing a simple cross entropy loss as following and the training is summarized in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4.2 Reverse ‣ 4 Continuously Augmented Discrete Diffusion (CADD) ‣ Continuously Augmented Discrete Diffusion model for Categorical Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>:</p>
<table id="S4.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boxed{{\mathcal{L}}_{\mathrm{CADD}}=\mathbb{E}_{t\sim\mathrm{Uniform}({1,...,T})}\mathbb{E}_{q({\bm{x}}_{t},{\bm{z}}_{t}\mid{\bm{x}}_{0})}\big[-\sum_{i:\,{\bm{x}}_{t}^{i}={\bm{m}}}\log p_{\theta}({\bm{x}}_{0}^{i}\mid{\bm{x}}_{t}^{i},{\bm{z}}_{t}^{i})\big]}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><menclose notation="box"><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>CADD</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Uniform</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.448em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒎</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒛</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></menclose><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boxed{{\mathcal{L}}_{\mathrm{CADD}}=\mathbb{E}_{t\sim\mathrm{Uniform}({1,...,T})}\mathbb{E}_{q({\bm{x}}_{t},{\bm{z}}_{t}\mid{\bm{x}}_{0})}\big[-\sum_{i:\,{\bm{x}}_{t}^{i}={\bm{m}}}\log p_{\theta}({\bm{x}}_{0}^{i}\mid{\bm{x}}_{t}^{i},{\bm{z}}_{t}^{i})\big]}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Note that we may add the MSE loss in equation <a href="#S4.E16" title="Equation 4.16 ‣ Lemma 1. ‣ 4.2 Reverse ‣ 4 Continuously Augmented Discrete Diffusion (CADD) ‣ Continuously Augmented Discrete Diffusion model for Categorical Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.16</span></a> to the above objective to more accurately estimate the exact variational lower bound. Empirically we find the simplified loss is more computationally efficient, thus we choose to use this loss for most of our experiments unless otherwise specified.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Sampling</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The sampling start from the last timestep <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> of the diffusion chain. Under the absorbing forward, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{T}\approx 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{T}\approx 0</annotation></semantics></math>, hence <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p({\bm{x}}_{T})=\delta_{{\bm{x}}_{T}={\bm{m}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒎</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p({\bm{x}}_{T})=\delta_{{\bm{x}}_{T}={\bm{m}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., all tokens are masked.
Since all positions are masked at <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, the continuous prior is
<math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p({\bm{z}}_{T}|{\bm{x}}_{T})=\prod_{i=1}^{n}\mathcal{N}\big({\bm{z}}_{T}^{i};\ {\bm{0}},\ {\bm{I}}_{d}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mn class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold"> 0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑰</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p({\bm{z}}_{T}|{\bm{x}}_{T})=\prod_{i=1}^{n}\mathcal{N}\big({\bm{z}}_{T}^{i};\ {\bm{0}},\ {\bm{I}}_{d}\big)</annotation></semantics></math>,
which matches the forward marginal at <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. For each timestep, given <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{x}}_{t},{\bm{z}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{x}}_{t},{\bm{z}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, the network
predicts</p>
<table id="S4.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta,i}(v)\ :=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\ p_{\theta}(\hat{\bm{x}}_{0}^{i}=v\mid{\bm{x}}_{t},{\bm{z}}_{t}^{(k)})\in\Delta^{V-1}\qquad\text{for each position }i." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>for each position </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta,i}(v)\ :=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}\ p_{\theta}(\hat{\bm{x}}_{0}^{i}=v\mid{\bm{x}}_{t},{\bm{z}}_{t}^{(k)})\in\Delta^{V-1}\qquad\text{for each position }i.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">For an unmasked position, the absorbing chain keeps <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{t-1}^{i}={\bm{x}}_{t}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{t-1}^{i}={\bm{x}}_{t}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> almost surely and
the continuous variable is deterministic <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{t-1}^{i}={\bm{z}}_{t}^{i}={\bm{w}}_{\theta}({\bm{x}}_{t}^{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{t-1}^{i}={\bm{z}}_{t}^{i}={\bm{w}}_{\theta}({\bm{x}}_{t}^{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. For a masked position, with probability <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1-\alpha_{t-1}}{1-\alpha_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1-\alpha_{t-1}}{1-\alpha_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>, it draws a clean token <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\sim\pi_{\theta,i}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\sim\pi_{\theta,i}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> to unmask it. If this masked position is unmasked in this step, the continuous latent <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{t-1}^{i}\leftarrow w_{\theta,v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{t-1}^{i}\leftarrow w_{\theta,v}</annotation></semantics></math>. If it remains masked, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{t-1}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{t-1}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> moves along the continuous diffusion trajectory <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{t-1}^{i}\sim\mathcal{N}\!\Big(\tilde{\bm{\mu}}_{t}\big(\hat{\bm{z}}_{0,\theta}^{i},\ {\bm{z}}_{t}^{i}\big),\ \tilde{\beta}_{t}{\bm{I}}_{d}\Big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.798em;" width="0.798em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>β</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝑰</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{t-1}^{i}\sim\mathcal{N}\!\Big(\tilde{\bm{\mu}}_{t}\big(\hat{\bm{z}}_{0,\theta}^{i},\ {\bm{z}}_{t}^{i}\big),\ \tilde{\beta}_{t}{\bm{I}}_{d}\Big)</annotation></semantics></math> following equation <a href="#S4.E13" title="Equation 4.13 ‣ Proposition 2 (Factorization of the true posterior). ‣ 4.2 Reverse ‣ 4 Continuously Augmented Discrete Diffusion (CADD) ‣ Continuously Augmented Discrete Diffusion model for Categorical Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.13</span></a>.
The full sampling process is shown in Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 4.2 Reverse ‣ 4 Continuously Augmented Discrete Diffusion (CADD) ‣ Continuously Augmented Discrete Diffusion model for Categorical Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Note the choice of <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{z}}_{0,\theta}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{z}}_{0,\theta}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> has two options:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S4.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E18.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\textbf{hard: }\ \hat{\bm{z}}_{0,\theta}={\bm{w}}_{\theta}(\hat{\bm{x}}_{0}),\hat{\bm{x}}_{0}=\argmax_{v}\pi_{\theta,i}(v)\quad\textbf{soft: }\hat{{\bm{z}}}_{0,\theta}:=\sum_{v}\pi_{\theta,i}(v)\,{\bm{w}}_{\theta,v}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">hard: </mtext><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">soft: </mtext><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>v</mi></munder><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{hard: }\ \hat{\bm{z}}_{0,\theta}={\bm{w}}_{\theta}(\hat{\bm{x}}_{0}),\hat{\bm{x}}_{0}=\argmax_{v}\pi_{\theta,i}(v)\quad\textbf{soft: }\hat{{\bm{z}}}_{0,\theta}:=\sum_{v}\pi_{\theta,i}(v)\,{\bm{w}}_{\theta,v}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4.18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These two choices are both valid to use depending on whether we are looking for mode-covering or mode-seeking behavior, i.e., better context localization or better diversity, respectively. In our main experiments we keep the hard option, and our empirical exploration in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 Ablation studies ‣ Appendix C Additional Experiment Results ‣ Continuously Augmented Discrete Diffusion model for Categorical Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a> justify these two choices could meet the demand of these two behavior. Moreover, although CADD may leverage multi-sample for the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> distribution estimation, for fair comparison with baselines, we keep <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=1</annotation></semantics></math> for most of our experiments. More detailed studies are also shown in the Appendix <a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 Ablation studies ‣ Appendix C Additional Experiment Results ‣ Continuously Augmented Discrete Diffusion model for Categorical Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Implementation</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We follow the common-used design of the model architecture to let <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> predict logits for categorical distribution. The discrete path follows earlier masked-diffusion setups: starting from <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, we mask a
subset of positions to obtain <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, embed the mixed sequence with the learnable table and form <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{disc}}={\bm{w}}_{\theta}({\bm{x}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>disc</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{disc}}={\bm{w}}_{\theta}({\bm{x}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. The only difference is the model needs to take an additional variable <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> input for the continuous embeddings. To achieve this, we first form the clean embeddings <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{0}={\bm{w}}_{\theta}({\bm{x}}_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒘</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{0}={\bm{w}}_{\theta}({\bm{x}}_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>, and then apply noise only at masked positions using the forward marginal equation <a href="#S4.E7" title="Equation 4.7 ‣ Proposition 1 (Timestep-𝑡 joint marginal factorization). ‣ 4.1 Forward ‣ 4 Continuously Augmented Discrete Diffusion (CADD) ‣ Continuously Augmented Discrete Diffusion model for Categorical Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a> to obtain <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. We fuse <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{disc}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>disc</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{disc}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> by element-wise addition <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{{\bm{z}}}_{t}:={\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{disc}}+{\bm{z}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>disc</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{{\bm{z}}}_{t}:={\bm{z}}_{\mathrm{disc}}+{\bm{z}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and feed <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{{\bm{z}}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{{\bm{z}}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to the backbone <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> to produce per-position logits.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we present experiments to validate the proposed CADD model through experiments on text, image, and code generation benchmarks. The evaluations are designed to assess the model’s performance across diverse data modalities and scales.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Text Generation</h3>

<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Experiment setting</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For text generation, we strictly follow the experimental setup of the Masked Diffusion Language Model (MDLM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sahoo et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, a common configuration for this task. We train our CADD models on the OpenWebText (OWT) dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gokaslan &amp; Cohen, 2019</a>)</cite>. Data is tokenized using the GPT-2 tokenizer with a vocabulary size of <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{V}|=50,257" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>50,257</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{V}|=50,257</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, and sequences are fixed to a length of <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\!=\!1,024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">=</mo><mn>1,024</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\!=\!1,024</annotation></semantics></math>. To be consistent with the baselines, we use a Discrete DiT backbone <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peebles &amp; Xie, 2023</a>)</cite> with approximately 168M parameters, and train with same number of iterations. All training hyper-parameters are identical to those in MDLM.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We mainly compare the performance with discrete diffusion baselines in terms of the generative quality, and our evaluation protocol strictly follows that of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2025)</a></cite>. We compare the performance against discrete diffusion baselines using two metrics: the MAUVE score (higher is better) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pillutla et al., 2021</a>)</cite> and generative perplexity (lower is better) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lou et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Further details on the evaluation setup are located in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Detailed Experiment Settings ‣ Continuously Augmented Discrete Diffusion model for Categorical Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F4.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01329v1/mauve_vs_T_enhanced.svg" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/197;" width="476" height="197"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F4.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01329v1/genppl_vs_T_enhanced.svg" id="S5.F4.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/197;" width="476" height="197"></object>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F4.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S5.F4.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Unconditional text generative evaluation of model trained on OpenWebText (OWT) data. All method are evaluated with 128, 256, 512 1024, and 4096 sampling steps. MAUVE (<span id="S5.F4.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Left Panel</span>, higher is better) and generative perplexity (<span id="S5.F4.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Right Panel</span>, measured using GPT2-Large, lower is better) are reported.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Main Results.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Evaluation. ‣ 5.1 Text Generation ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Continuously Augmented Discrete Diffusion model for Categorical Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> presents the results for unconditional text generation on the OpenWebText (OWT) dataset, comparing CADD with SEDD (absorb) and MDLM across a range of sampling steps <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="T\in\{" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\{</annotation></semantics></math>128, 256, 512, 1024, 4096<math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Within the range <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\leq 1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\leq 1024</annotation></semantics></math>, all models show improvement as the number of sampling steps increases. We can notice CADD demonstrates stronger and consistent gains as steps increase compared to SEDD and MDLM in terms of both metrics. Plotting the x-axis on a <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> scale reveals that the performance trend is approximately linear.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Extending the sampling process to <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=4096" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4096</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=4096</annotation></semantics></math> further demonstrates CADD’s scaling capabilities at inference time, as it continues to improve while the masked-only baselines stagnate or degrade. From <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=1024" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1024</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=1024</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4096" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4096</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4096</annotation></semantics></math>, CADD’s MAUVE score still increases by 0.3, and its generative perplexity is scored from 44.6 to 35.3. MDLM’s performance slightly worsens, which is consistent with the observation that mask-only diffusion models scale poorly with <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Overall, CADD consistently show performance gain across all tested number of sampling steps over the mask-only discrete diffusion models, validating the effectiveness of the proposed continuous-augmented space.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Computation</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With our design, the number of trainable parameters in the network is actually the same as MDMs, which is 168M for the used DiT architecture. We also measure the inference time for 5000 samples on 8 H100 GPUs, where both MDLM and CADD take 0.5h. When the number of samples used for <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\bm{z}}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\bm{z}}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is 1, i.e., <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=1</annotation></semantics></math>, the computation in the network is comparable since we only have extra computation in the forward and the fusion (add) operation. The computation cost increases linearly as <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> goes greater than 1.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure ltx_flex_table">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.T2.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_top" style="width:169.1pt;">
